<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-4" name=3D"story-4" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>4. The 2027 employer-cost outlook=
 increases demand for intervention=E2=80=94but raises the ROI bar</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Aon projected on August 20 that U.S. e=
mployer healthcare costs will increase 9.5% in 2027, pushing average cost abo=
ve $19,000 per employee. Aon characterized this as the fourth consecutive yea=
r of near-double-digit increases and said employers currently absorb more tha=
n 80% of plan costs. <a href=3D"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/aon-=
us-employer-health-care-costs-continue-multi-year-climb-projected-to-rise-9-5=
-in-2027--302855864.html" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">=
Aon, =E2=80=9CU.S. Employer Health Care Costs Continue Multi-Year Climb,=E2=
=80=9D August 20, 2026</a>. (<a href=3D"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-relea=
ses/aon-us-employer-health-care-costs-continue-multi-year-climb-projected-to-=
rise-9-5-in-2027--302855864.html?utm_source=3Dopenai" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;=
text-decoration:underline;">prnewswire.com</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Why it matters</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">If renewals approach that level, emplo=
yers will intensify pressure on commercial carriers and administrators to dem=
onstrate savings from high-performance networks, site-of-care steering, pharm=
acy controls, payment integrity and complex-case management. Fully insured pl=
ans face margin risk where pricing lags trend; self-funded employers bear the=
 cost directly.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Strategist implication</strong=
></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Demand for cost-management technology =
should rise, but buyers will be less tolerant of overlapping vendor fees and =
attribution-based savings claims. Solutions that connect intervention to allo=
wed-cost reduction, provider behavior or contract performance should gain sha=
re. Providers should expect more steerage, utilization controls and purchaser=
 scrutiny of facility-based pricing.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Actual renewal pricing and whether emp=
loyers respond primarily through contribution increases, benefit reductions o=
r more selective provider and pharmacy networks.</p>
